__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){Object.defineProperty(e,"__esModule",{value:!0}),e.meta=e.body=void 0;e.meta={slug:"why-position-matters-6max",title:"Why Position Matters in 6-Max Cash",summary:"Position isn’t just jargon—it’s the single biggest structural edge in hold’em. Here’s how it shapes every hand you play.",publishedAt:"2025-02-28",relatedLessonIds:["L1_open_raise_position_6max"],featureLobby:!0,relatedArticleSlugs:["building-preflop-opening-range","3bet-or-fold-stop-flat-calling","pot-odds-plain-english"]},e.body="## The hand that changed how I thought about position\n\nI was in the cutoff with A♣ J♦. The table had been tight; UTG folded, the next two folded, and it was on me. I opened to 2.5bb. The button called. Both blinds folded. Flop came K♠ 9♥ 4♦. I c-bet. He called. Turn 3♣. I checked. He bet. I folded.\n\nLater, the same hand—A♣ J♦—but this time I was on the button. UTG opened 2.5bb, two players called, and I called. Flop K♠ 9♥ 4♦. UTG bet, one fold, one call, and I called. Turn 3♣. UTG checked, the other player checked, and I bet. Both folded. I never had to show. Same cards; completely different outcome. The difference was **position**.\n\nThat’s not luck. It’s structure. In 6-max cash, position is the one edge that’s baked into the game before any card is dealt. If you don’t understand it, you’re giving away money every session.\n\n## What “position” actually means\n\n**Position** is simply where you sit relative to the dealer button. The button acts **last** on every postflop street (except preflop, when the blinds act last). The small blind acts first postflop; then the big blind; then UTG (under the gun), and so on, until the button.\n\nWhy does that matter? Because **the player who acts last has more information**. They see what everyone else did before they have to decide. They can check when others bet, bet when others check, and size their bets based on how the hand has already developed. The player who acts first is guessing. The player who acts last is responding.\n\nIn practice:\n\n- **Late position (cutoff, button)** — You get to open fewer hands but with a real edge when you do. You see the flop last, so you can value-bet, bluff, or give up with full knowledge of the action.\n- **Early position (UTG, UTG+1 / LJ)** — You have to be tighter. You’ll act first on every flop, turn, and river, so speculative hands that need to hit the flop hard become much harder to play profitably.\n- **The blinds** — You’re out of position against everyone. You pay a tax (the blind) and then often have to act first. That’s why we defend the big blind selectively and don’t overplay weak hands from the small blind.\n\nOne way to internalize it: **tighten in early position, loosen in late position**. It’s the first rule of 6-max preflop, and it exists because of the information gap that position creates.\n\n## The real cost of ignoring position\n\nThe leak I see most often is **opening too many hands from early position**. Suited aces, offsuit broadways, small pairs—they feel playable. And they are—from the button. From UTG or LJ, you’re going to miss the flop most of the time, and when you do, you’re the one who has to act first. You end up check-folding or betting into a range that has you crushed. Over thousands of hands, that adds up to a serious drag on your win rate.\n\nThe fix isn’t to memorize a chart and robotically follow it. It’s to **feel** why position matters: every time you’re last to act, you get a free look at what everyone else did. Every time you’re first to act, you’re guessing. So play fewer hands from early position and more from late position. Let structure work for you instead of against you.\n\n## Take it to the table\n\nIf you want to lock this in, train it. Our **Open-Raise Discipline by Position** lesson walks you through exactly which hands to open from each seat and why—so you stop bleeding EV in the highest-frequency spot in the game.\n\n[Practice open-raise discipline in Poker School](/lesson/L1_open_raise_position_6max) · [Head to the lobby](/lobby)"},1445,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){Object.defineProperty(e,"__esModule",{value:!0}),e.meta=e.body=void 0;e.meta={slug:"3bet-or-fold-stop-flat-calling",title:"3-Bet or Fold: Stop Flat-Calling Yourself Into Trouble",summary:"That smooth-call feels safe. It’s also one of the biggest preflop leaks. Here’s how to take the lead instead.",publishedAt:"2025-02-28",relatedLessonIds:["L2_face_open_3bet_call_fold"],featureLobby:!0,relatedArticleSlugs:["why-position-matters-6max","building-preflop-opening-range","pot-odds-plain-english"]},e.body="## The call that felt right and was wrong\n\nVillain opened from the cutoff. I looked down at K♥ Q♥ in the big blind. I thought: *strong hand, good odds, I’ll call and see a flop.* I called. The flop came 9♦ 6♣ 2♥. I checked. He bet. I had nothing—no pair, no draw—and I folded. Again and again, the same pattern: I’d flat with “good” hands, miss the flop, and give up. Or I’d hit and have no idea if I was ahead or bluffing. I was playing **passive** in a game that rewards **initiative**.\n\nIt took a losing month and a coach’s blunt question—“Why are you calling with KQ?”—to see it. KQ is a fine hand. But from the big blind against a cutoff open, **calling** is often the worst of three options. Folding is defensible if the open is tight. **3-betting** is often best: you take the lead, you build the pot when you’re ahead, and you don’t hand the initiative to someone who gets to act last on every street.\n\nThat’s the idea behind **3-bet or fold**: in many spots, flat-calling is a leak. You’re either strong enough to 3-bet (for value or as a bluff) or you should fold. The middle road—calling and hoping to hit—bleeds EV.\n\n## Why flat-calling bleeds\n\nWhen you flat preflop:\n\n- You’re **out of position** (unless you’re on the button). You’ll act first on the flop, turn, and river. Your opponent gets to react to you.\n- You **cap your range**. It’s hard for your opponent to put you on a monster when you just called. So when you do hit, you get less value.\n- You **invite multiway pots**. Other players behind might call too. Now you’re in a hand with a marginal holding and two or three opponents. Your equity shrinks; the complexity grows.\n\nNone of that means “never call.” There are spots where calling is correct—for example, in the big blind getting a good price, or on the button with a hand that plays well postflop in position. But the default for many hands facing an open should be **3-bet or fold**, not “call and see.”\n\n## What “3-bet or fold” actually means\n\n**3-bet** = the second raise in a hand (open is the first raise, your raise is the 3-bet). When you 3-bet, you’re saying: “I’m not just along for the ride; I’m contesting this pot.”\n\nYou 3-bet for two reasons:\n\n1. **Value** — You have a hand that’s ahead of the opener’s range (e.g. QQ+, AK). You want more money in the pot.\n2. **Bluff / pressure** — You have a hand that’s not quite value but plays well if you get the lead (e.g. some suited broadways, suited aces). You 3-bet to fold out better hands and take the pot down when they fold, or to play a big pot in position when they call.\n\nIf your hand isn’t strong enough to 3-bet for value and isn’t a good 3-bet bluff, the alternative is often **fold**. That’s uncomfortable. It feels like “giving up.” But giving up with K8o from the small blind versus an UTG open isn’t weak—it’s correct. You’re avoiding a situation where you’re likely to lose money.\n\n## The mental shift\n\nThe shift is from “I have a decent hand, I’ll call” to “I have a hand that either wants the lead or doesn’t belong in the hand.” Once you think in **buckets**—3-bet for value, 3-bet as a bluff, or fold—you stop filling the middle bucket (flat-call) with hands that don’t belong there. Your preflop becomes cleaner and your postflop becomes easier, because you’re the one with the initiative more often.\n\n## Train the decision\n\nOur **3-Bet / Call / Fold** lesson puts you in the exact spot: someone opens, you have a real hand, and you have to choose. No theory without practice—you’ll see the feedback and lock in the buckets.\n\n[Do the 3-Bet / Call / Fold lesson](/lesson/L2_face_open_3bet_call_fold) · [Play at the table](/lobby)"},1446,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){Object.defineProperty(e,"__esModule",{value:!0}),e.meta=e.body=void 0;e.meta={slug:"how-to-use-hand-replay",title:"How to Use Hand Replay to Fix Leaks",summary:"The hands you forget are the ones that cost you the most. Replay turns every session into a chance to improve.",publishedAt:"2025-02-28",featureLobby:!0,relatedArticleSlugs:["practice-to-tables","why-position-matters-6max","pot-odds-plain-english"]},e.body="## The hand I couldn’t stop replaying in my head\n\nI’d lost a big pot. Top pair, decent kicker, and I’d called down on a scary board. When my opponent showed the straight, I was sure I’d misplayed it. But by the next day, I couldn’t remember the exact runout or what I’d been thinking. I had a feeling—*I should have folded*—but no way to check. So I repeated the same kind of call the next week and lost again.\n\nThat’s when I started **replaying hands**. Not in my imagination—actually rewatching the hand, step by step, with the cards and the action in front of me. The first time I did it, I saw something obvious: I’d had a clear fold on the turn. The pot was small, my opponent had represented strength on two streets, and I was calling hoping to hit one of six outs. It wasn’t close. I’d just refused to let go. Replay made the leak visible. The next time a similar spot came up, I folded. That one change paid for itself in a single session.\n\nReplay isn’t about beating yourself up. It’s about **turning experience into feedback**. The hands you don’t review are the ones that slip away. The ones you replay become lessons.\n\n## Why replay works\n\nWhen you play live, the hand is over in minutes. You’re in the moment—stack sizes, table talk, fatigue, tilt. Afterward, memory is fuzzy and biased. You remember the bad beat; you forget the three times you called too loose on the turn.\n\nReplay removes the noise. You see:\n\n- **Exact action** — Who bet what, when. No “I think he bet half pot.”\n- **Board and stacks** — You can judge pot odds and implied odds with cold numbers.\n- **Your options** — At each decision point, you can pause and ask: what else could I have done?\n\nThat’s how patterns show up. Maybe you’re calling too much on the river. Maybe you’re underbluffing when you have the lead. Maybe you’re overplaying weak top pair from out of position. One hand is an anecdote. Ten replayed hands are data.\n\n## How we built replay for you\n\nHere you can replay **your last hand** as soon as you finish it. No export, no notepad—just open the lobby and hit **Replay last hand**. You’ll see the same table, the same cards, the same action. Scrub through street by street. Pause at the decision you want to review. Ask yourself: *Would I do the same again?*\n\nWe also ship a **Community Hand**: a curated hand (with snapshots) that you can step through even if you’ve never played a hand on the site. Use it to see how a tough spot plays out from start to finish—then try your own hands the same way.\n\n## A simple replay habit\n\nYou don’t need to replay every hand. Aim for:\n\n1. **Your biggest pot won and your biggest pot lost** — What did you do right? What did you do wrong?\n2. **Any hand that felt “weird”** — If you weren’t sure at the table, you’re not sure now. Replay it and decide what you’d do next time.\n3. **One hand per session** — Even one replay per day builds the habit. In a month you’ll have 30 hands you’ve actually learned from.\n\nOver time, you’ll start to see the same mistakes. Those are your leaks. Fix one at a time.\n\n## Replay last hand or try the Community Hand\n\n[Replay last hand](/lobby) (from the lobby) · [Open a community hand](/lobby) (same place—try the walkthrough hand) · [Back to the tables](/lobby)"},1447,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){Object.defineProperty(e,"__esModule",{value:!0}),e.meta=e.body=void 0;e.meta={slug:"pot-odds-plain-english",title:"Pot Odds in Plain English",summary:"You don’t need to be a mathematician to use pot odds. Here’s the one idea that changes how you call or fold.",publishedAt:"2025-02-28",relatedLessonIds:["L5_cbet_dry_board"],featureLobby:!0,relatedArticleSlugs:["why-position-matters-6max","building-preflop-opening-range","3bet-or-fold-stop-flat-calling"]},e.body="## The call I made that still bothers me\n\nFlop: K♠ 9♥ 4♦. I had 9♦ 8♦—middle pair and a backdoor flush draw. Villain bet about two-thirds of the pot. I thought: *I have a pair, I might improve, I’ll call.* I called. Turn was a blank. He bet again. I called again. River was another blank. He bet. I folded. I’d put in two big bets with a hand that was either behind or drawing thin. When I finally learned **pot odds**, I ran the hand back. My call on the flop needed to be right way too often to be profitable. I’d been “feeling” my way through. The math said fold.\n\nPot odds aren’t magic. They’re just the **price the pot is offering you** compared to the **chance you have of winning**. If the price is good, you call. If it’s not, you fold. Once you see that, a lot of “close” decisions become clear.\n\n## What pot odds actually are\n\n**Pot odds** = how much is in the pot right now versus how much you have to put in to call.\n\nExample: There’s 100 in the pot. Villain bets 50. So the pot is now 150, and you have to call 50. Your **pot odds** are 150 : 50, or **3 : 1**. For every 1 you put in, you’re playing for 3 (your call plus the 150). So you need to win **1 out of 4 times** (1 / (3+1) = 25%) just to break even. If you win more than 25% of the time, the call is profitable. If you win less, it’s a fold.\n\nYou don’t have to do this in your head to the decimal. The habit that matters is: **before you call, ask “how often do I need to win?”** If the bet is half the pot, you need to win one in three times (about 33%). If the bet is the size of the pot, you need to win one in two (50%). The bigger the bet you face, the more often you need to win—so you need a stronger hand or a better draw.\n\n## Connecting odds to your hand\n\nPot odds tell you the **break-even win rate**. Your hand (and the board) tell you your **actual win rate**—your **equity**. If your equity is higher than the break-even %, calling is +EV. If it’s lower, calling is -EV.\n\nWith a **draw**, equity is easier to approximate. A flush draw (9 outs) has about 18% to hit on the flop (one card to come) and about 36% with two cards to come. So if the pot is offering you 3 : 1 (25% needed), a flush draw with two cards to come is a call in a vacuum. If the pot is offering you 2 : 1 (33% needed), that same draw is a fold unless you get extra value when you hit (implied odds).\n\nWith **made hands** (pair, two pair), you’re not “drawing”—you’re either ahead or behind. You estimate how often you’re ahead vs. the range you put your opponent on. If you’re ahead 40% of the time and the pot odds say you need to win 30%, you call. If you’re ahead 20% of the time and you need 40%, you fold.\n\n## The one habit that changes everything\n\nBefore you call a bet, **pause**. How much is in the pot? How much is the bet? Roughly what do I need to win—one in three? One in four? Then: **Do I have that?** (Either the equity now, or the chance to improve plus implied odds.) If you’re not sure, lean fold. Calling “to see what happens” is how pot odds get violated over and over.\n\n## Practice with structure\n\nOur flop and postflop lessons put you in spots where pot odds matter—calling or folding with a draw or a marginal hand. You get the numbers in front of you and the feedback right after.\n\n[Train postflop decisions in Poker School](/lesson/L5_cbet_dry_board) · [Play at the table](/lobby)"},1448,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){Object.defineProperty(e,"__esModule",{value:!0}),e.meta=e.body=void 0;e.meta={slug:"building-preflop-opening-range",title:"Building a Preflop Opening Range (6-Max)",summary:"Which hands to open and from where isn’t random. Here’s how to build a range that makes money instead of giving it back.",publishedAt:"2025-02-28",relatedLessonIds:["L1_open_raise_position_6max"],featureLobby:!0,relatedArticleSlugs:["why-position-matters-6max","3bet-or-fold-stop-flat-calling","pot-odds-plain-english"]},e.body="## From “I’ll play anything that looks good” to a real range\n\nI used to open based on mood. Ace-ten? Sure. King-jack offsuit from early position? Why not. I had a vague sense that position mattered, but I didn’t have a **range**—a set of hands I’d open from each seat. So I’d open too much from UTG and get squeezed or outplayed postflop. Or I’d open too little from the button and leave value on the table. My win rate was flat. I was working hard and going nowhere.\n\nThe change came when I sat down and wrote out, seat by seat, what I’d open. Not from a book—from first principles: *In early position I have to act first on every street, so I need hands that can stand that pressure. In late position I act last, so I can open wider and steal more.* I tightened up front and loosened in back. Within a few weeks, my preflop felt calmer. I was in fewer marginal spots and in more spots where I had a clear edge. That’s the power of **building a range** instead of playing by instinct.\n\n## What an “opening range” is\n\nYour **opening range** (or RFI—raise first in) is the set of hands you’re willing to open (first to put money in) from each position when everyone has folded to you. It’s not a fixed list you memorize once. It’s a **framework**: early position = tighter, late position = wider, and the button is your widest spot because you have position on everyone postflop.\n\nRough idea for 6-max:\n\n- **UTG / LJ (early)** — Only your strongest hands: big pairs, big aces, strong broadways. You’re going to act first on the flop, turn, and river, so you want hands that can win without perfect flops.\n- **CO (cutoff)** — A bit wider. You have position on the blinds and only UTG/LJ left to act. Add more suited connectors and suited aces, and some offsuit broadways.\n- **BTN (button)** — Widest. You have position on everyone. You can open for value and to steal. This is where you add small pairs, suited gappers, and more speculative hands.\n- **SB** — Often a separate strategy (open or fold, sometimes limp in some games). Many players use a “min-raise or fold” or “open or fold” approach from the small blind with a range between CO and BTN width.\n\nYou don’t have to get it perfect. You have to **stop opening the same hands from every seat**. Tighten in front, loosen in back. That alone fixes a huge chunk of preflop leaks.\n\n## Why “just play good hands” isn’t enough\n\n“Good hands” depend on position. AJo from the button is a clear open. AJo from UTG is often a fold in tough games—you’re going to be out of position and dominated by better aces and bigger pairs. So the **same hand** is in your range in one seat and out of it in another. If you don’t define that, you’ll either over-open early (the most common leak) or under-open late (leaving money on the table).\n\nBuilding a range also forces you to think in **combos** and **frequency**. You’re not just “playing tight” or “playing loose.” You’re playing a percentage of hands from each seat, and that percentage goes up as you get closer to the button. That’s the structure. The heart of it is: **fewer hands from early position, more from late.**\n\n## How to build yours\n\n1. **Start from the button** — What do you open on the button when it’s folded to you? Write down a list (or use a range chart). That’s your widest range.\n2. **Work backward** — From the cutoff, remove the bottom of your button range. From LJ, remove more. From UTG, keep only the strongest hands. You’ll end up with a ladder: tightest at UTG, widest at BTN.\n3. **Use one source of truth** — A coach, a chart, or a training lesson. Don’t mix three different systems. Pick one and stick to it until it’s automatic.\n4. **Review and adjust** — If you’re getting 3-bet too much from early position, you might still be too wide. If you never get action from the button, you might be too tight. Ranges aren’t set in stone; they’re a baseline you refine with experience.\n\n## Lock it in with practice\n\nOur **Open-Raise Discipline by Position** lesson walks you through exactly which hands to open from each seat and grades your choices. You’ll see the feedback and build the habit.\n\n[Train your opening range in Poker School](/lesson/L1_open_raise_position_6max) · [See all lessons](/lessons) · [Play at the table](/lobby)"},1449,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){Object.defineProperty(e,"__esModule",{value:!0}),e.meta=e.body=void 0;e.meta={slug:"practice-to-tables",title:"From Practice to Tables: Getting the Most From Poker School",summary:"Lessons and replay only pay off when you connect them to real play. Here’s how to build a loop that actually improves your game.",publishedAt:"2025-02-28",featureLobby:!0,relatedArticleSlugs:["how-to-use-hand-replay","why-position-matters-6max","building-preflop-opening-range"]},e.body="## The gap between knowing and doing\n\nI’d done the drills. I knew I should 3-bet more and call less. I could recite pot odds. But at the table, in the moment, I kept doing the same things—calling when I should have raised, folding when I should have called. The knowledge was there. The **transfer** wasn’t. It wasn’t until I started treating **lessons**, **replay**, and **live play** as one loop that anything changed.\n\nThe idea is simple: **learn a little, play a little, review a little.** Repeat. That loop turns Poker School into something that actually moves your win rate.\n\n## The loop that works\n\n**1. Learn (lessons)**  \nDo one lesson or one concept. Not the whole curriculum in a day—one thing. For example: “Open-raise discipline by position.” Finish the lesson. Get the feedback. Understand why the right answer is right.\n\n**2. Play (tables)**  \nGo to the lobby and play. While you’re playing, **focus on that one thing**. If you trained open-raise discipline, then at the table your job is to open the right hands from the right seats. You’ll forget sometimes. That’s fine. The goal is to create real situations where the lesson applies.\n\n**3. Review (replay)**  \nAfter the session—or after a specific hand—replay. Use **Replay last hand** or rewatch the hand that felt messy. Did you do what the lesson said? If not, what would you do next time? You’re not grading yourself harshly; you’re closing the loop between “what I learned” and “what I did.”\n\nThen repeat. Next concept. Next session. Next replay.\n\n## Why this beats “just playing” or “just studying”\n\n**Just playing** — You reinforce whatever you already do. If you have a leak, you’ll keep doing it. Experience alone doesn’t fix leaks; it deepens ruts.\n\n**Just studying** — You get better at the lesson, not at the table. Without play, you never build the reflex. Without replay, you never see where you still slip.\n\n**Loop** — You introduce one change (lesson), you practice it in real time (play), and you check whether it stuck (replay). That’s how habits form. One concept at a time, with real stakes and real feedback.\n\n## Keep it small\n\nDon’t try to fix everything in a week. Pick **one** lesson or one article. For example:\n\n- “This week I’m tightening my UTG opens.”\n- “This week I’m 3-betting or folding instead of flat-calling with KQ.”\n- “This week I’m checking pot odds before I call a bet.”\n\nOne focus. Play with that in mind. Replay the hands where it mattered. Next week, pick the next thing.\n\n## Start the loop\n\n[Open Poker School](/lessons) and pick one lesson.  \n[Play at the table](/lobby) and focus on that one thing.  \n[Replay last hand](/lobby) from the lobby when you’re done.\n\nYou’ve got the content. Now make it part of your play."},1450,[]);
